With the `blocksInterruptParagraphs` option, a block-level construct may
interrupt a paragraph, so no blank line is required before it. The `i` flag
enables the option. This trades away hard-wrap friendliness (design goal #7)
but keeps the other goals, including uniform composition (#8).

A list may follow a paragraph with no blank line:

```i
hello
- a
- b
.
<p>hello</p>
<ul>
<li>
a
</li>
<li>
b
</li>
</ul>
```

A sublist needs no blank line either; indentation alone determines nesting:

```i
- a
  - b
  - c
.
<ul>
<li>
a
<ul>
<li>
b
</li>
<li>
c
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
```

Uniform composition (goal #8): the identical sequence above, placed inside a
block quote, produces the identical nested structure. This is the case that
CommonMark gets wrong (commonmark/commonmark-spec#634).

```i
> - a
>   - b
>   - c
.
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>
a
<ul>
<li>
b
</li>
<li>
c
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
```

Plain consecutive lines (no block marker) still form a single paragraph with a
soft break; only block markers interrupt:

```i
this is a
wrapped paragraph
.
<p>this is a
wrapped paragraph</p>
```

A block quote may follow a paragraph with no blank line:

```i
text
> quote
.
<p>text</p>
<blockquote>
<p>quote</p>
</blockquote>
```

The cost of dropping goal #7: a line that looks like a list marker starts a
list. This is consistent in all contexts; it is only surprising when
hard-wrapping, which this option does not aim to support.

```i
1865.
.
<ol start="1865">
<li>
</li>
</ol>
```
